Q: Conditional import of Java Vector API A: <p>No, you can’t conditionally import <code>jdk.incubator.vector</code> in one source file and still run that same class on Java 8. Imports are compile-time only. If you reference:</p>
<pre><code>import jdk.incubator.vector.*;
</code></pre>
<p>the code must be compiled with a JDK that has that module. Also, Java 8 can’t load classes compiled for Java 17. The usual options are keep Java 8 code as the default, and put the Java 17 Vector API version in a version-specific source set:</p>
<pre><code>src/main/java/...
src/main/java17/...
</code></pre>
<p>JAR layout:</p>
<pre><code>com/example/MyAlgorithm.class              // Java 8 fallback
META-INF/versions/17/com/example/MyAlgorithm.class  // Java 17 vector version
</code></pre>
<p>Java 8 ignores <code>META-INF/versions/17</code>, while Java 17 loads it.</p>
<pre><code>my-lib-core      // Java 8 compatible
my-lib-vector17  // Java 17 + Vector API
</code></pre>
<p>Then the app picks the right dependency. Possible, but honestly messy. Java 8 must never load any class that directly references <code>jdk.incubator.vector</code>. Example:</p>
<pre><code>interface Processor {
    void process(float[] input);
}

class ScalarProcessor implements Processor {
    public void process(float[] input) {
        // Java 8-safe code
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Java 17-only class:</p>
<pre><code>class VectorProcessor implements Processor {
    // uses jdk.incubator.vector here
}
</code></pre>
<p>Load <code>VectorProcessor</code> reflectively only on Java 17+. For app code, this is often the most practical choice if the Vector API is important. Also, “incubator” doesn’t mean toy. It means the API is not final yet and may change across JDK versions. So keep it isolated behind a small abstraction. A good design:</p>
<pre><code>public API
  -&gt; Processor interface
      -&gt; Scalar Java 8 implementation
      -&gt; Vector Java 17 implementation isolated separately
</code></pre>

Q: In SOLID, what is the distinction between SRP and ISP? (Single Responsibility Principle and Interface Segregation Principle) A: <p>As it was previously mentioned:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>SRP is a class-level concept; to comply with it, you should answer the question: <code>Does this class do too many different things?</code></p>
</li>
<li><p>ISP is an interface-level concept; to comply with it, you should answer the question: <code>Does this interface force a client to know about extra things it doesn't care about?</code></p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>To illustrate this, let's imagine that you have to create simple document management logic that allows you to read and to save a document.</p>
<p>Then, you start by creating an interface first to declare all the functions the functionality has (the code is written in C#):</p>
<pre><code>public interface IDocumentTaskService
{
    Task SaveDocumentAsync(Document document, CancellationToken token);

    Task&lt;string&gt; ReadDocumentAsync(Guid documentId, CancellationToken token);
}
</code></pre>
<p>At the first glance, it looks quite logical since it groups everything that is related to the document and hides it behind the abstraction. But the main problem is that <strong>it violates the ISP principle</strong>: the client code that will use this interface implementation will be able to access much more functions that it needs. And in the future, the <code>IDocumentTaskService</code> could become much bigger, exacerbating the problem and making the code harder to unit test.</p>
<p>For some time, the <code>IDocumentTaskService</code> implementation will not violate the single responsibility principle by having only one reason to change, as long as it only works with document-domain entities:</p>
<pre><code>public class DocumentTaskService : IDocumentTaskService
{
    public Task SaveDocumentAsync(Document document, CancellationToken token)
    {
        // Do some logic to save the document...
    }

    public Task&lt;string&gt; ReadDocumentAsync(Guid documentId, CancellationToken token)
    {
        // Do some logic to save the document...
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>This illustrates that your <strong>code could comply with SRP principle, but would potentionally violate the ISP principle</strong>.</p>
<p>To fix this, you should define separate interfaces like it is shown below:</p>
<pre><code>// This interface contains methods only for the write logic. 
public interface IDocumentWriterService
{
    Task SaveDocumentAsync(Document document, CancellationToken token);
}
</code></pre>
<pre><code>public interface IDocumentReaderService
{   
    Task&lt;string&gt; ReadDocumentAsync(Guid documentId, CancellationToken token);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Now there are separate interfaces, one per function you need to manage the document processing.</p>
<p>But what if we have an implementation of the <code>IDocumentWriterService</code> like the following?</p>
<p>Now the DocumentWriterService does many different things: it performs some validation, and serialization before saving the document:</p>
<pre><code>public class DocumentWriterService : IDocumentWriterService
{
    public async Task SaveDocumentAsync(Document document, CancellationToken token)
    {
        // Responsibility 1: Validation
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(document.Title))
        {
            throw new ArgumentException(&quot;Document Title is empty.&quot;);
        }

        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(document.Id &lt;= 0))
        {
            throw new ArgumentException(&quot;Document ID is not set.&quot;);
        }

        // Responsibility 2: Serialization
        string json = JsonSerializer.Serialize(document);

        // Responsibility 3: Building the path
        string path = Path.Combine(AppContext.BaseDirectory, $&quot;{document.Id}-{document,Title}-{DateTime.Now}.json&quot;);

// Responsibility 4: Saving file

        await File.WriteAllTextAsync(path, json, token);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Now <strong>it follows the ISP, but violates the SRP principle</strong>. To fix that, we'll need to <strong>move the responsibilities to the separate dependancies</strong>:</p>
<pre><code>public interface IDocumentValidationService
{
    bool IsValid(Document document);
}

public interface IJsonSerialisationService
{
    string Serialize(object data);
}

public interface IFileService
{
    Task WriteAllTextAsync(string content, string filePath, CancellationToken token);
}

public interface IDocumentPathBuilder
{
    string BuildPath(string content, string filePath, CancellationToken token);
}
</code></pre>
<p>and use them, as follows:</p>
<pre><code>public class DocumentWriterService : IDocumentWriterService
{
    public DocumentWriterService( /* initialize the dependencies using DI */)
    {}

    public async Task SaveDocumentAsync(Document document)
    {
        _validationService.Validate(document);

        string json = _serialisationService.Serialize(document);

        string path = _documentPathBuiler.BuildPath(document);

        await _fileService.WriteAllTextAsync(path, json);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>This approach follows both ISP and SRP principles and makes it easy to implement these interfaces individually, change their implementations on the fly using DI containers, modify them, and unit test them.</p>
<p>Hope this helps you to understand the difference between ISP and SRP.</p>

Q: How to add multiple Flip Cards using html A: <p>So first thing is that you just copied code from W3Schools and you copied it <code>WRONG</code>!</p>
<p><strong>First</strong> HTML... you have 2 <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> more in your code then you are supposed to have.
<br /><br />
<strong>Second</strong> thing is that your <strong>CSS total mess</strong>. Just use CSS that W3Schools gives you and everything works fine.</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.flip-card {
  background-color: transparent;
  width: 300px;
  height: 300px;
  perspective: 1000px;
}

.flip-card-inner {
  position: relative;
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  text-align: center;
  transition: transform 0.6s;
  transform-style: preserve-3d;
  box-shadow: 0 4px 8px 0 rgba(0,0,0,0.2);
}

.flip-card:hover .flip-card-inner {
  transform: rotateY(180deg);
}

.flip-card-front, .flip-card-back {
  position: absolute;
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  -webkit-backface-visibility: hidden;
  backface-visibility: hidden;
}

.flip-card-front {
  background-color: #bbb;
  color: black;
}

.flip-card-back {
  background-color: #2980b9;
  color: white;
  transform: rotateY(180deg);
}
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;div class=&quot;flip-card&quot;&gt;
